CHS = []

CHS.append(CH(
 "Traits and generics",
 ("Traits", []),
 ["A trait describes behaviour a type can have, like a job description. Many types can implement the same trait in their own way, and functions can accept anything that has the behaviour they need.",
  "Generics let you write one function or type that works for many types, with trait bounds saying what those types must be able to do.",
  "Trait objects (dyn) let you store different types together and choose the right method at run time."],
 [("Trait", "A set of method signatures a type can promise to provide."), ("impl Trait for Type", "Gives a type the behaviour."), ("Generic", "Code written for any type T."), ("Trait bound", "A requirement such as T: PartialOrd."), ("dyn", "A trait object: any type with the trait, decided at run time."), ("Default method", "A method with a body inside the trait.")],
 ["A trait is a promise of methods.", "Each type implements the trait its own way.", "Generics with bounds avoid writing the same function many times.", "Box<dyn Trait> mixes different types in one list.", "Missing a promised method is error E0046."],
 [T("Imagine a job posting for a speaker. It says: must be able to speak. A dog, a cat and a cow could all apply, each speaking in a different way. A trait is that job posting."),
  R('trait Speak {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Dog;\nstruct Cat;\n\nimpl Speak for Dog {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { String::from("Woof") }\n}\nimpl Speak for Cat {\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { String::from("Meow") }\n}'),
  F("rs-trait.svg", "One trait, several types, each implementing it in its own way."),
  T("If an impl forgets a method the trait requires, the compiler says not all trait items are implemented. A trait can also provide default methods with bodies that implementors get for free and may override."),
  T("Generics. Suppose you write largest for a list of i32. Then you need it for characters. Copying the function is wasteful. A generic function says: for any type T that can be compared, here is the code."),
  R('fn largest<T: PartialOrd>(v: &[T]) -> &T {\n    let mut best = &v[0];\n    for x in v {\n        if x > best {\n            best = x;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}'),
  T("T: PartialOrd is a trait bound. Without it, the compiler complains that it cannot compare two values of an unknown type T. Bounds are how you tell Rust exactly what your generic code needs, no more and no less."),
  T("Trait objects. Sometimes you want one list holding different types that share a trait, such as a Vec of shapes. Use Box<dyn Shape>. Each element knows its real type, and calling area() runs the right code. Generics decide at compile time and are fastest; dyn decides at run time and is more flexible."),
  T("Common built-in traits you can get with derive: Debug (printing), Clone (explicit copy), Copy (implicit copy), PartialEq (==), PartialOrd (<, >), Default. And Display, which you implement yourself, controls how {} prints your type.")],
 [("What is a trait?", ["A set of methods a type can promise", "A kind of loop", "A variable", "A file"], 0, "A job description."),
  ("What does T: PartialOrd mean?", ["T must be comparable with < and >", "T is a number", "T is a string", "T is a loop"], 0, "A trait bound."),
  ("What does Box<dyn Shape> allow?", ["Different shape types in one collection", "Faster math", "Shorter names", "Nothing"], 0, "Run-time choice."),
  ("What error appears if an impl misses a required method?", ["E0046", "E0382", "E0499", "E0597"], 0, "Not all trait items implemented."),
  ("Which derive lets you print with {:?}?", ["Debug", "Display", "Print", "Clone"], 0, "Debug.")],
 [("Trait", "Promise of methods"), ("Bound", "Say what a generic needs"), ("dyn", "Mix types at run time"), ("derive", "Free Debug, Clone, PartialEq ...")],
 [MATCH("Match the idea", "Pair each term with its meaning.", [("Trait", "promised behaviour"), ("Generic", "code for any type"), ("Bound", "requirement on a generic"), ("dyn Trait", "run-time chosen type")]),
  FILL("Traits", "A {{trait}} lists methods. You give a type the behaviour with {{impl}} Trait for Type. A {{generic}} function works for many types. A trait object is written {{dyn}}."),
  ORDER("Define and use a trait", "Order the steps.", ["Define the trait", "Implement it for each type", "Write code that takes the trait", "Call the method"])]))

CHS.append(CH(
 "Lifetimes",
 ("Lifetimes", []),
 ["A lifetime is the stretch of a program during which a reference is valid. Rust checks that no reference outlives the thing it points to, so there are no dangling pointers.",
  "Most of the time Rust works lifetimes out by itself. Sometimes, usually when a function returns a reference chosen from several inputs, you must add a name such as 'a to say how they relate.",
  "Lifetime annotations never change how long data lives. They only describe relationships so the compiler can check them."],
 [("Lifetime", "How long a reference is valid."), ("Dangling reference", "A reference to data that no longer exists."), ("'a", "A named lifetime parameter."), ("Elision", "Rust filling in obvious lifetimes for you."), ("'static", "The whole run of the program."), ("Outlive", "To remain valid longer than something else.")],
 ["A reference must not outlive its data.", "E0597: borrowed value does not live long enough.", "E0106: missing lifetime specifier on returned references.", "Name lifetimes to link inputs to outputs.", "Return owned values when in doubt."],
 [T("Think of a library card that is valid only while the library is open. If the library closes while you still hold the card, the card is useless. A lifetime is that opening time, and Rust checks that your references never outlive it."),
  R('fn main() {\n    let r;\n    {\n        let x = 5;\n        r = &x;\n    }\n    println!("{}", r);\n}'),
  F("rs-life.svg", "The reference r lives longer than x, so the program is rejected."),
  T("This is rejected. x lives only inside the inner braces. After they close, x is dropped, but r still points at it. The compiler says x does not live long enough. The fix is to use r while x is still alive, or to keep x in a wider scope."),
  T("Functions. Consider longest, which returns whichever of two strings is longer. The result is a reference, but to which input? Rust cannot know, and refuses with missing lifetime specifier. You tell it with a lifetime name."),
  R("fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {\n    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }\n}"),
  T("Read it as: for some lifetime a, both inputs live at least as long as a, and the result lives for a too. So the result is valid as long as both inputs are. The annotation does not stretch anything. It describes what is true so the compiler can check callers."),
  T("Elision. For simple functions Rust fills in lifetimes. With exactly one reference parameter, the output reference gets that lifetime. With &self methods, the output gets self's. That is why fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str needs no annotation, but longest with two inputs does."),
  T("Structs that borrow. A struct holding a reference needs a lifetime too: struct Excerpt<'a> { part: &'a str }. It means an Excerpt cannot outlive the text it points into."),
  T("Returning a reference to a local variable is always wrong: the local is dropped when the function ends. Rust reports missing lifetime specifier or cannot return reference to local variable. Return an owned String instead. A good rule: if lifetimes are fighting you, ask whether the function should own its data rather than borrow it.")],
 [("What is a lifetime?", ["How long a reference is valid", "How long a program runs", "A kind of loop", "A type of number"], 0, "Validity of a reference."),
  ("What does E0597 mean?", ["Borrowed value does not live long enough", "Moved value", "Two mutable borrows", "Missing trait"], 0, "Dangling reference."),
  ("Do lifetime annotations change how long data lives?", ["No", "Yes", "Only for strings", "Only for structs"], 0, "They only describe."),
  ("Why does longest(x: &str, y: &str) -> &str fail?", ["Rust cannot tell which input the result borrows from", "Strings cannot be compared", "It is too long", "Missing main"], 0, "Needs a named lifetime."),
  ("Which fix works for returning a reference to a local String?", ["Return the String itself", "Add 'static", "Add more &", "Use unwrap"], 0, "Owned data.")],
 [("Lifetime", "How long a reference is valid"), ("'a", "Names the relationship"), ("Elision", "Simple cases need nothing"), ("Own it", "Return owned data when stuck")],
 [MATCH("Match the term", "Pair each with its meaning.", [("Lifetime", "validity period of a reference"), ("'a", "a named lifetime"), ("Dangling", "pointing at data that is gone"), ("Elision", "Rust infers it for you")]),
  FILL("Lifetimes", "A reference must not {{outlive}} its data. Annotations are written like {{'a}}. They describe but do not {{change}} how long data lives. Simple functions get lifetimes by {{elision}}."),
  ORDER("Fix a dangling reference", "Order the idea.", ["Notice the reference outlives its value", "Find where the value is dropped", "Use the reference before that point", "Or keep the value in a wider scope"])]))

CHS.append(CH(
 "Closures and iterators",
 ("Iterators", []),
 ["A closure is a small anonymous function that can use variables from around it. An iterator is something that yields a sequence of values one at a time.",
  "Together they let you describe what you want, such as keep the odd ones, square them, add them up, instead of writing a loop that spells out every step.",
  "Iterators are lazy: nothing happens until something consumes them, and they compile down to loops as fast as handwritten ones."],
 [("Closure", "An anonymous function written |x| x + 1."), ("Capture", "A closure using a variable from its surroundings."), ("Iterator", "Something that produces values one by one."), ("Adapter", "A step like map or filter that makes a new iterator."), ("Consumer", "A step like sum or collect that produces a final result."), ("Lazy", "Doing nothing until asked for a value.")],
 ["Closures are |args| body and can capture variables.", "map transforms; filter keeps; sum, collect, count consume.", "Iterator chains are lazy.", "collect needs to know the target type.", "iter borrows; into_iter consumes."],
 [T("A closure is a function without a name that you can hand around like a note. Where a normal function cannot see the variables of the code that calls it, a closure can capture them."),
  R('fn main() {\n    let n = 5;\n    let add = |x: i32| x + n;\n    println!("{}", add(7));\n}'),
  T("The closure add uses n from outside. The pipes hold the parameters. You will mostly write closures as short arguments to iterator methods."),
  T("An iterator is like a conveyor belt. Items ride along, and you attach machines: a filter that removes items, a map that changes each one. Finally a consumer at the end collects what arrives."),
  F("rs-iter.svg", "A source, two adapters and a consumer."),
  R('fn main() {\n    let total: i32 = (1..=10)\n        .filter(|n| n % 2 == 1)\n        .map(|n| n * n)\n        .sum();\n    println!("{}", total);\n}'),
  T("Here the range is the source. filter keeps the odd ones. map squares them. sum adds them to 165. Notice that the machines only start moving when sum asks for values. That is laziness, and it means an iterator chain does no wasted work."),
  T("Common adapters: map, filter, enumerate (adds positions), zip (pairs two iterators), take, skip, rev, chain. Common consumers: sum, count, min, max, fold (your own accumulator), any, all, find, position, collect."),
  R('fn main() {\n    let words = vec!["hi", "yo"];\n    let up: Vec<String> = words.iter().map(|w| w.to_uppercase()).collect();\n    println!("{:?}", up);\n}'),
  T("collect builds a collection from an iterator. It needs to know which one, so you give the type: Vec<String>, HashMap, String, or even Result<Vec<_>, _>, which stops at the first error."),
  T("iter() borrows the items. into_iter() takes them. iter_mut() lets you change them in place. Which one you use follows the ownership rules from earlier chapters: if you still need the collection afterwards, borrow it.")],
 [("What does |x| x + 1 define?", ["A closure", "A struct", "A loop", "A macro"], 0, "An anonymous function."),
  ("Which keeps only items that satisfy a test?", ["filter", "map", "sum", "zip"], 0, "Filter keeps."),
  ("Which is a consumer?", ["sum", "map", "filter", "rev"], 0, "It produces a final value."),
  ("What does lazy mean?", ["Nothing runs until a value is requested", "It is slow", "It skips items", "It runs twice"], 0, "On demand."),
  ("What must you tell collect?", ["The target collection type", "The number of items", "The speed", "Nothing"], 0, "Vec, String, HashMap ...")],
 [("Closure", "|x| x + n"), ("Adapter vs consumer", "map/filter make iterators; sum/collect finish"), ("Lazy", "Chains do nothing until consumed"), ("collect", "Name the type you want")],
 [MATCH("Match the step", "Pair each with its job.", [("map", "transform each item"), ("filter", "keep matching items"), ("sum", "add everything up"), ("collect", "gather into a collection")]),
  FILL("Chains", "A {{closure}} is written with pipes. {{filter}} keeps items that pass a test. {{map}} changes each item. {{sum}} adds them up. Chains are {{lazy}}."),
  ORDER("A pipeline", "Order the pipeline from source to result.", ["Start with the range 1..=10", "filter the odd numbers", "map to squares", "sum to get 165"])]))

CHS.append(CH(
 "Smart pointers and concurrency",
 ("Smart pointers", []),
 ["Smart pointers are types that act like references but also own data and add abilities. Box puts a value on the heap. Rc lets several owners share a value and counts them. RefCell lets you change a value through a shared handle by checking the borrowing rules at run time.",
  "Rust also helps with threads. The same ownership and borrowing rules that prevent memory bugs also prevent data races: if your program compiles, two threads cannot accidentally write the same data at once.",
  "This chapter closes the course with a map of where to go next."],
 [("Box", "A pointer to one value on the heap."), ("Rc", "Reference counted pointer: many owners, single thread."), ("Arc", "Atomic Rc: many owners across threads."), ("RefCell", "Interior mutability: borrow rules checked at run time."), ("Thread", "A unit of work that runs alongside others."), ("Channel", "A queue for sending values between threads.")],
 ["Box<T> owns one heap value; needed for recursive types.", "Rc<T> shares ownership; Rc::clone adds an owner.", "RefCell<T> allows change through a shared reference, checked at run time.", "Channels move data between threads.", "Rc is not thread safe: use Arc."],
 [T("Box. A recursive type such as a list that contains another list would have infinite size, since each contains the next. A Box is a fixed-size pointer to the heap, which breaks the loop."),
  R('enum List {\n    Cons(i32, Box<List>),\n    Nil,\n}\n\nuse List::{Cons, Nil};\n\nfn main() {\n    let l = Cons(1, Box::new(Cons(2, Box::new(Nil))));\n}'),
  T("Rc. Sometimes one value truly has several owners, like a node in a graph. Rc keeps a count of owners. Rc::clone does not copy the data, it adds one to the count. When the count reaches zero, the data is freed."),
  F("rs-pointers.svg", "Box owns one value. Rc counts owners. RefCell allows changes through a shared handle."),
  R('use std::rc::Rc;\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Rc::new(String::from("shared"));\n    println!("{}", Rc::strong_count(&a));\n    let b = Rc::clone(&a);\n    println!("{}", Rc::strong_count(&a));\n}'),
  T("RefCell. Shared handles are read only. RefCell moves the borrow check to run time so you can change the contents anyway: borrow() reads and borrow_mut() writes. If you break the rules (two borrow_mut at once) the program panics instead of failing to compile. The common pairing Rc<RefCell<T>> means shared and changeable."),
  T("Threads. thread::spawn runs a closure in another thread. join waits for it to finish. A channel lets threads talk: the sender tx sends, the receiver rx receives, and sending moves the value, so only one thread owns it at a time. In this lab, threads run one after another so output is predictable, but the compiler rules are exactly the real ones."),
  F("rs-threads.svg", "A worker sends a message through a channel to the main thread."),
  T("Rc is not allowed across threads because its counter is not atomic. If you try, the compiler says Rc cannot be sent between threads safely. Swap in Arc. For shared changeable data across threads, the standard pairing is Arc<Mutex<T>>."),
  T("Where next? Read the official book, The Rust Programming Language, which is free online. Practise with small exercises such as Rustlings. Try cargo new on your own computer and build a command-line tool. Explore crates on crates.io. Rust rewards patience: the compiler errors you saw here are the same ones professionals fix every day.")],
 [("Why does a recursive enum need Box?", ["Otherwise its size would be infinite", "To make it faster", "To print it", "To make it public"], 0, "Box is a fixed-size pointer."),
  ("What does Rc::clone do?", ["Adds an owner and increments the count", "Copies the data deeply", "Moves the value", "Deletes the value"], 0, "Cheap."),
  ("What does RefCell check?", ["Borrow rules, at run time", "Types, at compile time", "Spelling", "Speed"], 0, "Panics on a violation."),
  ("Which should you use across threads instead of Rc?", ["Arc", "Box", "RefCell", "Vec"], 0, "Atomic counting."),
  ("What does sending a value through a channel do?", ["Moves it to the receiver", "Copies it silently", "Shares it mutably", "Deletes it"], 0, "Ownership moves.")],
 [("Box", "One owner on the heap"), ("Rc / Arc", "Shared ownership with a count"), ("RefCell", "Change through a shared handle"), ("Channel", "Send, do not share")],
 [MATCH("Match the pointer", "Pair each with its job.", [("Box", "heap value, recursive types"), ("Rc", "many owners, one thread"), ("RefCell", "run-time borrow checking"), ("Arc", "many owners across threads")]),
  FILL("Pointers", "A {{Box}} holds one value on the heap. {{Rc}} counts owners. {{RefCell}} checks borrowing at run time. Across threads use {{Arc}}."),
  ORDER("Share and change", "Order the steps to share a changeable list.", ["Create RefCell::new(vec![])", "Wrap it in Rc::new", "Rc::clone for each owner", "borrow_mut().push(...) to change it"])]))
